---
import BaseLayout from '../../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const alternateLanguages = [
  { lang: 'it', url: 'https://www.retro-gamers.it/classici-giocabili-oggi/policy/' },
  { lang: 'en', url: 'https://www.retro-gamers.it/en/playable-classics/policy/' },
  { lang: 'x-default', url: 'https://www.retro-gamers.it/classici-giocabili-oggi/policy/' }
];

const languageSwitch = {
  url: '/classici-giocabili-oggi/policy/'
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Playable Classics Policy"
  description="Policy for Playable Classics on Retro-Gamers.it: legal criteria, sources, internal downloads, accounts and removal requests."
  image="/og-image.jpg"
  canonical="https://www.retro-gamers.it/en/playable-classics/policy/"
  lang="en"
  languageSwitch={languageSwitch}
  alternateLanguages={alternateLanguages}
>
  <div class="legal-page">
    <div class="container">

      <section class="legal-hero">
        <span>Download policy</span>
        <h1>Playable Classics</h1>
        <p>
          An editorial section for games, demos, homebrew and retro materials that
          can be shared in a clear, documented and rights-conscious way.
        </p>
      </section>

      <article class="legal-content">

        <p class="legal-updated">
          Last updated: 15 July 2026
        </p>

        <h2>1. Purpose of the section</h2>

        <p>
          “Playable Classics” is meant to help readers rediscover games and retro
          materials in an organized, verifiable and legal way. It is not meant to be
          a generic archive of files found online or a shortcut to unclear sources.
        </p>

        <p>
          The goal is to build public editorial pages with historical context,
          practical instructions, verifiable sources and, when Retro-Gamers.it
          believes there is a reasonable basis to do so, internal downloads reserved
          for registered users.
        </p>

        <p>
          This policy describes the editorial criteria used by the website. It is not
          legal advice and does not replace advice from a qualified professional.
        </p>

        <h2>2. What the section offers</h2>

        <p>
          Each page may combine editorial content, practical information and technical
          references. Depending on the material covered, it may include:
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>an editorial guide with historical context and usage notes;</li>
          <li>external links to official sources, author pages, licenses or related projects;</li>
          <li>internal downloads hosted by Retro-Gamers.it, when redistribution appears to be allowed;</li>
          <li>notes about license, source, verification and redistribution;</li>
          <li>information about recommended emulators, interpreters or tools.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          Where possible, pages distinguish between editorial content, external links
          and files downloaded directly from the website.
        </p>

        <h2>3. What may be hosted or referenced</h2>

        <p>
          Retro-Gamers.it may host or reference only materials with a reasonable and
          documentable basis for distribution. Depending on the case, a page may include
          an internal download, a practical guide, an official source or a reference to
          the author’s page.
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>official freeware, when third-party redistribution appears to be allowed;</li>
          <li>homebrew released for free or authorized by its author;</li>
          <li>open source projects, according to the applicable license;</li>
          <li>demo and shareware packages that are redistributable under known conditions;</li>
          <li>emulator configurations, presets, technical files, checklists or guides created by Retro-Gamers.it;</li>
          <li>official external sources used as reference, license, author page or project page.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>4. What will not be hosted</h2>

        <p>
          Retro-Gamers.it will not host protected commercial material or legally
          ambiguous files. Lack of sale or support does not automatically make a game
          redistributable.
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>protected BIOS, Kickstart, firmware or system ROM files;</li>
          <li>commercial console ROMs;</li>
          <li>commercial arcade dumps;</li>
          <li>ISO files, archives or copies of commercial games without authorization;</li>
          <li>manuals, scans, artwork or protected assets without permission;</li>
          <li>material described only as abandonware without a clear legal basis.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>5. Categories and definitions</h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Freeware</strong> means software distributed for free by the rights
          holder. Free does not always mean freely redistributable: conditions must be
          checked case by case.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Open source</strong> means software distributed with source code and
          an open license. The license defines what can be distributed, changed or
          included.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Homebrew</strong> means modern productions for historical platforms
          or inspired by them. They can be free, commercial or open source: the author’s
          choice always matters.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Demos</strong> and <strong>shareware</strong> may have been created
          to circulate freely, but not every package is automatically redistributable
          by a third-party site. A clear source is still required.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Abandonware</strong> is an informal term. It is not a legal status.
          A game may be hard to find or no longer sold and still remain protected.
        </p>

        <h2>6. Admission principle</h2>

        <p>
          The rule is simple: if we cannot clearly explain why a file is distributable,
          we do not host it.
        </p>

        <p>
          When the available information is not enough to host a file with reasonable
          confidence, the page may remain an editorial guide, mention an official source
          or link to the author’s page, without offering an internal download.
        </p>

        <h2>7. Internal downloads and accounts</h2>

        <p>
          Some pages include files hosted directly by Retro-Gamers.it. In these cases,
          the internal download is available only to registered and authenticated users,
          in order to avoid permanent public links, reduce misuse and keep minimal
          control over how the service operates.
        </p>

        <p>
          Download links may be temporary and generated only after server-side checks.
          Users must not bypass access checks, share temporary links or use files in a
          way that conflicts with the applicable conditions.
        </p>

        <p>
          Internal downloads are free of charge unless explicitly stated otherwise in
          the future. The presence of a download does not transfer rights in the work
          and does not automatically authorize further redistribution by the user.
        </p>

        <h2>8. Sources, licenses and limits of verification</h2>

        <p>
          Each page should state, based on the information available, the legal status
          of the content, the relevant source or authorization and any redistribution
          notes.
        </p>

        <p>
          Verification is carried out in good faith using the sources available at the
          time the page is published or updated. It cannot absolutely guarantee that
          every piece of information is complete, definitive or still up to date.
        </p>

        <p>
          Freeware, open source, homebrew status or availability on other websites is
          not considered, by itself, sufficient proof for internal redistribution:
          license, source, authorization and specific conditions always matter.
        </p>

        <h2>9. User responsibility</h2>

        <p>
          Users are responsible for how they use materials downloaded from the website
          or reached through external links. They must respect licenses, rights holder
          conditions and the laws applicable in their country.
        </p>

        <p>
          Retro-Gamers.it does not guarantee that a file will be compatible with every
          system, emulator or configuration, or that it will remain permanently available.
        </p>

        <h2>10. Rights holders and removal requests</h2>

        <p>
          If a rights holder believes that a piece of content has been included by
          mistake, they can write to <a href="mailto:info@retro-gamers.it">info@retro-gamers.it</a>
          and request a review. Retro-Gamers.it will evaluate the request and may change,
          suspend or remove the affected content, including files, links, editorial
          notes or entire pages.
        </p>

        <p>
          A request should preferably indicate the affected content, the reason for the
          report, the relationship with the rights being claimed and a valid contact for
          any clarification.
        </p>

        <h2>11. Availability and service changes</h2>

        <p>
          Retro-Gamers.it may update, suspend or remove downloads, pages, metadata,
          external links and editorial references at any time, for technical, editorial,
          legal or security reasons.
        </p>

        <p>
          Publication of a page or download is not a guarantee of permanent availability.
        </p>

        <h2>12. Privacy and minimal download data</h2>

        <p>
          For downloads reserved for registered users, Retro-Gamers.it may process
          account data and a minimal technical log connected to the download request,
          useful for security, maintenance, abuse prevention and service quality.
        </p>

        <p>
          For more information about personal data processing, please see the
          <a href="/en/privacy-policy/">Privacy Policy</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>13. Editorial spirit</h2>

        <p>
          Retro-Gamers.it wants to preserve, explain and contextualize videogame
          history. “Playable Classics” follows that intention: helping readers play and
          understand, without turning the site into a grey archive or an indiscriminate
          collection of files.
        </p>

      </article>

    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
